# Loom documentation

Loom is a literate programming framework. You write a document — prose and code together, in whatever order reads best — and Loom composes the code out of it into real source files. The prose is not commentary wrapped around a program. It is half of the program: the layer a person reads to learn what the code does and why.

These pages are the manual. They begin with a five-minute quick start, then cover the format itself and the commands that act on it. Read them in order the first time; after that, each page stands on its own.

The manual is itself a Loom corpus. Every page you read here is a chapter of prose that `loom weave` rendered, which means the documentation cannot drift from the tool that builds it.

